      "q": "What does E stand for in Ohm's law?",
      "a": "Electromotive force, the older name for voltage. E and V mean the same thing: E = I × R and V = I × R are the same equation."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I enter milliamps or kilohms?",
      "a": "Convert to base units first: 20 mA is 0.02 A, 4.7 kΩ is 4700 Ω and 250 mW is 0.25 W. Most answers that are off by a factor of 1,000 come from a missed conversion."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does Ohm's law work for LEDs?",
      "a": "Not for the LED itself, because an LED's current is not proportional to its voltage. Apply Ohm's law to the series resistor instead: the voltage across it is the supply minus the LED's forward voltage. The LED resistor calculator does exactly that."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the calculator only take two values?",
      "a": "Voltage, current, resistance and power are linked by two equations, so any two of them fix the other two. If you type a third value the calculator asks you to clear one, because the three might contradict each other."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I work out how much power a resistor dissipates?",
      "a": "Use P = I² × R or P = E² / R. For example, 9 V across 470 Ω gives I = 9 / 470 = 19.1 mA and P = 81 / 470 = 0.172 W, which is fine for a ¼ W resistor."
